[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"ASP.NET Core MVC and Web API Tutorial"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"ASP.NET Core is a cross-platform, high-performance, open-source framework for building modern, cloud-based, internet-connected applications. It allows you to build web applications and APIs using the Model-View-Controller (MVC) architectural pattern and Web API for creating RESTful services. This tutorial will guide you through the basics of building both MVC applications and Web APIs using C#."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":".NET SDK (latest version)"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Visual Studio or Visual Studio Code"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Basic understanding of C#"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating an ASP.NET Core MVC Project"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Open Visual Studio and create a new project. Select 'ASP.NET Core Web App (Model-View-Controller)' and click 'Next'. Configure your project name and location, then click 'Create'. Choose the target framework (e.g., .NET 7.0) and click 'Create'."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the MVC Structure"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"The MVC pattern separates the application into three interconnected parts:"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Model: Represents the data of the application."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"View: Displays the data to the user."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Controller: Handles user input and updates the model and view."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating a Controller"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a new controller by right-clicking the 'Controllers' folder in your project and selecting 'Add' -> 'Controller'. Choose 'MVC Controller - Empty' and name it 'HomeController'."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

namespace YourProjectName.Controllers
{
    public class HomeController : Controller
    {
        public IActionResult Index()
        {
            return View();
        }
    }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating a View"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a new view by right-clicking the 'Views' folder, then 'Home', and selecting 'Add' -> 'View'. Name it 'Index.cshtml'. Add some HTML to the view."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Home Page";
}

<div class="text-center">
    <h1 class="display-4">Welcome</h1>
    <p>Learn about <a href="https://docs.microsoft.com/aspnet/core">building Web apps with ASP.NET Core</a>.</p>
</div>"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Running the Application"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Run the application by pressing F5 or clicking the 'Start' button in Visual Studio. You should see the 'Welcome' page displayed in your browser."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating an ASP.NET Core Web API"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a new project in Visual Studio. Select 'ASP.NET Core Web API' and click 'Next'. Configure your project name and location, then click 'Create'. Choose the target framework (e.g., .NET 7.0) and click 'Create'."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Web API Structure"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"Web APIs are designed to expose data and functionality through HTTP. They typically use RESTful principles."},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating a Controller for the Web API"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a new controller by right-clicking the 'Controllers' folder in your project and selecting 'Add' -> 'Controller'. Choose 'API Controller - Empty' and name it 'ProductsController'."},{"order":29,"type":"code","text":"using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

namespace YourProjectName.Controllers
{
    [ApiController]
    [Route("[controller]")]
    public class ProductsController : ControllerBase
    {
        [HttpGet]
        public ActionResult<IEnumerable<string>> Get()
        {
            return new string[] { "product1", "product2" };
        }
    }
}"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"The '[ApiController]' attribute indicates that this is an API controller. The '[Route("[controller]")]' attribute defines the route for the controller. The '[HttpGet]' attribute specifies that the 'Get' method handles HTTP GET requests."},{"order":31,"type":"subheading","text":"Testing the Web API"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"Run the application. You can use tools like Postman or Swagger (if enabled) to test the API endpoint. Navigate to 'https://localhost:[port]/products' in your browser or Postman. You should see a JSON response containing the array of products."},{"order":33,"type":"subheading","text":"Adding Data Models"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a 'Models' folder in your project. Add a new class named 'Product.cs' to represent a product."},{"order":35,"type":"code","text":"namespace YourProjectName.Models
{
    public class Product
    {
        public int Id { get; set; }
        public string Name { get; set; }
        public decimal Price { get; set; }
    }
}"},{"order":36,"type":"subheading","text":"Using the Data Model in the API"},{"order":37,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modify the 'ProductsController' to return a list of 'Product' objects."},{"order":38,"type":"code","text":"using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using YourProjectName.Models;
using System.Collections.Generic;

namespace YourProjectName.Controllers
{
    [ApiController]
    [Route("[controller]")]
    public class ProductsController : ControllerBase
    {
        [HttpGet]
        public ActionResult<IEnumerable<Product>> Get()
        {
            var products = new List<Product>
            {
                new Product { Id = 1, Name = "Product 1", Price = 19.99m },
                new Product { Id = 2, Name = "Product 2", Price = 29.99m }
            };
            return products;
        }
    }
}"},{"order":39,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":40,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial provided a basic introduction to building ASP.NET Core MVC applications and Web APIs. You can further explore topics like data access, authentication, authorization, and deployment to build more complex and robust applications."}]
